22:43.482 --> 22:47.094
Yes. So we kind of maximize that as much as possible while we

22:47.594 --> 22:50.723
have the funds before they get spent. Is that a fair statement? Yes. Okay.

22:51.285 --> 22:54.625
Thank you. All right, this will be the last

22:54.641 --> 22:58.110
one, which is looking at stormwater fund. Um,

22:58.126 --> 23:01.931
stormwater fund, we made some kind of big changes

23:02.044 --> 23:05.592
operationally on this, um, in the last few

23:05.657 --> 23:08.949
fiscal years, wherein we kind of have a— we added more

23:09.449 --> 23:12.754
personnel, um, really on this department. And then, um,

23:12.963 --> 23:16.737
some of those, I feel like it's just— it's tied to

23:17.237 --> 23:20.751
the vehicles that they need and other operational, um, stuff that they're working on.

23:21.120 --> 23:24.669
So that's why the expenditure is a little bit higher compared

23:25.169 --> 23:28.651
to last fiscal year. But in terms of revenue collection,

23:29.100 --> 23:32.777
we're still in the line, about $500,000,

23:32.794 --> 23:36.679
um, when it comes to the actual collection. So there's no concern about that.

23:36.968 --> 23:40.517
So here's some of the key items

23:40.645 --> 23:44.081
for this budget update. I'm happy to answer

23:44.113 --> 23:45.205
any question you might have.

23:54.891 --> 23:59.496
My— I can't remember in our last quarterly meeting, but I was

23:59.592 --> 24:03.606
wondering if it would be possible— here we're looking at revenue and

24:04.106 --> 24:08.166
expenses, which is helpful from an income statement standpoint. Usually those

24:08.439 --> 24:11.731
types of presentations are also paired with a balance sheet.

24:12.297 --> 24:16.485
of, because we have different assets

24:16.985 --> 24:20.405
in the city. Mm-hmm. And people are surprised, like, well, we've got $30 million over

24:20.905 --> 24:23.408
here, why don't we just use that to pay for stuff, you know, out of

24:23.441 --> 24:27.199
our general fund? But those dollars are actually allocated for specific projects.

24:27.569 --> 24:31.311
Gotcha. And I would love to just see a quick balance sheet of

24:31.811 --> 24:35.279
what those dollars are, what projects they're kind of assigned to. Again, I don't know

24:35.779 --> 24:38.957
if that would be a heavy lift to maintain that each quarter and just report

24:39.021 --> 24:42.406
back to the council. Because, you know,

24:42.470 --> 24:47.280
as an example, from the utility sale, we have like $3 million that

24:47.616 --> 24:51.079
have yet to be spent. We've had them for quite

24:51.579 --> 24:54.798
some time. And I don't know, you know,

24:54.814 --> 24:59.175
it might be worth us like using those funds for use for residents versus

24:59.223 --> 25:02.381
having it just sit in a portfolio earning, you know, interest.

25:02.445 --> 25:05.411
Gotcha. But if we have that kind of in the front of the council's mind

25:05.911 --> 25:08.617
that These dollars actually need to get used for the public.

25:09.499 --> 25:12.400
That would be a helpful reminder, I think, for us. Gotcha.

25:12.464 --> 25:15.157
That would be maybe one addition I would add to the presentation.

25:15.510 --> 25:18.716
Okay. For me personally, I just want to chime in on the

25:19.216 --> 25:22.339
budget side of things. It's kind of a little bit unique when it comes to

25:22.627 --> 25:25.737
local government we're in. What we do is we just,

25:26.234 --> 25:30.578
like, we focus on the fiscal year and pretty much whatever is your revenue

25:31.078 --> 25:34.262
and expenditure and where you are. But in terms of the balance sheet information,

25:34.342 --> 25:38.360
I probably will relay to Kimberly. I don't know, Kimberly, if you have any input

25:38.634 --> 25:42.668
on that one. Yeah. So when it comes to the balance

25:43.168 --> 25:46.672
sheet and identifying in the planning side, that's something that is done

25:47.108 --> 25:50.805
once a year when you're identifying and doing those capital projects.

25:53.061 --> 25:57.726
So as you know, we bring the financial statements forward to the council annually

25:57.758 --> 26:01.316
once the are done and audited and so forth. When we're in our budget and

26:01.816 --> 26:04.918
our planning phases with things, we're identifying here's what the resources are,

26:05.192 --> 26:09.155
here's what they're currently allocated to. When we're doing this update

26:09.171 --> 26:12.490
and these items, it is specifically related to the budget as it

26:12.990 --> 26:16.969
is adopted and what our spend and our plan is in conjunction

26:17.469 --> 26:17.742
with the fiscal year.

26:21.558 --> 26:22.526
Thank you.

26:27.006 --> 26:29.002
Okay, thank you, Terrence. Appreciate it.

26:30.276 --> 26:34.081
The next item, uh, 1B, is a discussion airport feasibility study

26:34.581 --> 26:37.145
results, and this is going to be exciting.

26:39.000 --> 26:42.109
Abby's going to lead us off. Hello, good afternoon,

26:43.176 --> 26:46.458
council member— no, there we go. Good afternoon, council members and mayor.

26:47.056 --> 26:50.289
Um, I appreciate and I'm very excited for today and what

26:50.789 --> 26:53.430
we're getting ready to do introduce the results of our feasibility study,

26:53.863 --> 26:57.671
I want to do just a quick introduction of who's here with us. They've been

26:58.201 --> 27:01.108
part of this study. This is Steve Domino,

27:02.136 --> 27:05.655
and then we have Clint Harper with Future Flight Global, and then Paul Dameron

27:06.155 --> 27:09.590
with Dameron Consulting. He represents the city and our interests.

27:10.442 --> 27:13.864
Just as a recap before I turn the time over to Steve, I just

27:14.364 --> 27:17.703
want to talk about the reasoning why we're doing this and why Eagle

27:17.719 --> 27:21.043
Mountain. As you know, we have a need for about

27:21.543 --> 27:25.238
30,000 jobs in Cedar Valley to take care of our population.

27:26.307 --> 27:29.456
Most of our people were working at the Tyson plant,

27:29.537 --> 27:32.783
and they work in tech, and those are 2 industries that

27:33.283 --> 27:34.688
seem to be going through a lot of layoffs lately.

27:36.732 --> 27:40.420
So what we're trying to do is create an area that will create jobs

27:40.452 --> 27:44.060
for us, and mainly it's these high-paying jobs, because the more high-paying jobs

27:44.092 --> 27:47.681
that work in these industries, industries like tech, the more people will live here

27:47.729 --> 27:51.162
in Eagle Mountain and work here in Eagle Mountain. And so that's our goal with

27:51.662 --> 27:53.793
this, is like, how can we do it? So we've consulted with a number of

27:53.825 --> 27:57.034
people who are saying that people who are in computer tech or software and all

27:57.534 --> 28:01.125
that stuff that are currently living in Eagle Mountain and going to Lehi

28:01.625 --> 28:05.232
or Salt Lake City can actually— those jobs translate really well into something in the

28:05.376 --> 28:09.179
Advanced Air Mobility Hub. So we're looking at that. We're hoping that by now

28:09.323 --> 28:14.088
keeping people here in Eagle Mountain, We won't have a 90% daytime

28:14.280 --> 28:17.601
leave of all of our residents, so that'll also help bring more

28:17.713 --> 28:21.708
retail, restaurants, and commercial development to Eagle Mountain.

28:21.901 --> 28:24.756
And so it's just, it's just really we're trying to help with the road problem,

28:26.216 --> 28:28.928
all the people on the roads, and then also trying to get more things into

28:28.960 --> 28:32.184
our city here. So why Eagle Mountain is

28:32.232 --> 28:35.216
a great place? Because we are close to the Wasatch Front,

28:36.019 --> 28:39.630
we have access to a 130,000 university and tech students,

28:40.032 --> 28:43.356
and we have a lot of free— a lot of open airspace.

28:43.468 --> 28:47.032
So that's not encumbered by the Provo and Salt Lake City airports.

28:47.434 --> 28:50.645
So we've been talking about this a lot out in the community,

28:50.934 --> 28:54.804
and everybody's really excited across the United States because there's nothing like this that's

28:55.304 --> 28:58.577
being done in the Intermountain West. And also the really great thing is, it's like,

28:58.641 --> 29:01.756
this is an emerging industry. It's not something that's untested.

29:01.869 --> 29:05.246
It is tested. But it's going to have a long shelf life. So it's not

29:05.746 --> 29:07.749
going to be something that's just going to go away in 10 years. It'll be

29:08.249 --> 29:11.430
here for a long time. So I'll turn over the time to Steve

29:11.788 --> 29:13.626
now to give us the results of the study.

29:41.677 --> 29:41.983
Good evening.

29:45.378 --> 29:48.096
Uh, today's agenda, what we want to do is we want to give a quick

29:48.209 --> 29:51.404
project overview about what the project entails.

29:51.452 --> 29:54.932
Uh, I'll turn it over to Clint Hart here, uh, for a

29:55.432 --> 29:58.688
couple of minutes to talk about the concept of advanced air mobility,

29:59.185 --> 30:03.292
and he'll also review some of the market opportunities for here

30:03.792 --> 30:07.351
in Eagle Mountain City. And then we'll talk about general facility

30:07.448 --> 30:11.956
requirements that are required for both airports

30:12.456 --> 30:16.352
as well as aviation hubs, different types of facilities that you might want to consider

30:16.852 --> 30:20.026
here for Eagle Mountain. And then I'll review the next steps that we would

30:20.058 --> 30:23.994
recommend that you follow. Thank you. follow through with to keep the project moving.

30:25.510 --> 30:29.262
So the project goal is to prepare a feasibility study to determine

30:29.310 --> 30:33.401
whether advanced air mobility hub could

30:33.433 --> 30:37.284
be developed here in Eagle Mountain City while supporting the

30:37.300 --> 30:40.893
long-term economic growth and compatibility with the surrounding

30:40.990 --> 30:44.444
community. At the beginning

30:44.460 --> 30:47.128
of the study, there were 3 general objectives.

30:48.350 --> 30:51.665
For developing an aviation facility in Eagle Mountain City.

30:52.067 --> 30:56.056
One was to integrate military operations

30:56.153 --> 31:00.526
if possible, adopt the concept

31:01.026 --> 31:04.786
of advanced air mobility because it would be an initial phase

31:04.902 --> 31:08.611
of development, and then the

31:09.111 --> 31:12.519
last thing is to try to get it included in the national

31:12.535 --> 31:17.134
plan of Integrated Airport Systems. This is a system

31:17.166 --> 31:21.489
of airports that the Federal Aviation manages that identifies which

31:21.602 --> 31:24.836
airports across the country are eligible for

31:25.336 --> 31:28.980
federal funds for investment and maintenance of airports.

31:30.778 --> 31:34.984
In addition to the general objectives, we identified

31:35.000 --> 31:39.189
6 guiding principles that we wanted this aviation hub

31:39.735 --> 31:43.460
to address. One was we need to make sure that

31:43.572 --> 31:47.715
the facilities are in compliance with all aviation

31:48.341 --> 31:51.697
standards. Second is we want to make sure that there's

31:51.761 --> 31:54.587
opportunity that the facility can grow.

31:55.534 --> 31:59.227
What you may do in your first step may not be your ultimate

31:59.727 --> 32:02.920
vision, and so you need to be able to have that— develop that ultimate

32:03.420 --> 32:06.695
vision and make sure that the facilities that you plan for today are going to

32:07.195 --> 32:10.552
be able to grow into the future. We want to make sure also

32:11.052 --> 32:15.826
that the land— that there's opportunity to maximize and

32:16.196 --> 32:19.464
optimize the use of the land. This involves ensuring

32:19.641 --> 32:23.473
that the land is used efficiently as well

32:23.586 --> 32:26.525
as being compatible with the neighboring community.

32:27.923 --> 32:31.471
We wanted to make sure that the aviation

32:31.487 --> 32:34.788
hub has the capacity to serve

32:34.821 --> 32:38.348
many different types of operations: military,

32:39.092 --> 32:41.838
advanced air mobility, drones,

32:42.505 --> 32:44.277
fixed-wing aircraft, and so forth.

32:45.895 --> 32:49.449
And we wanted to make sure that it— the hub is identified

32:49.465 --> 32:53.327
as a niche facility in the industry so it can better attract

32:53.749 --> 32:57.573
industries and investors in the airport. And then

32:57.911 --> 33:01.311
finally, we want to make sure that the aviation hub

33:01.811 --> 33:05.017
is financially sustainable. What we mean by that is that

33:05.081 --> 33:09.030
it ultimately will generate sufficient revenues

33:09.386 --> 33:12.756
that it will not require general fund revenues to

33:13.031 --> 33:16.591
continue its operation. With that, I'd like

33:17.091 --> 33:20.560
to turn some time over to Clint to talk about

33:21.060 --> 33:23.357
the concept of advanced air mobility. Thank you,

33:26.077 --> 33:29.245
Awesome. Great. Well, it's a pleasure to be here, council members, mayor,

33:29.278 --> 33:32.605
and members of the community. Before we get into

33:32.621 --> 33:35.455
the nuts and bolts of what we're talking about here, I think it's important to

33:35.520 --> 33:38.853
understand what is advancing mobility because there is a lot of

33:39.353 --> 33:42.522
hype around the industry and it gets easy to confuse, you know,

33:42.602 --> 33:46.620
what is advancing mobility, what is not advancing mobility, and what it means to the

33:47.120 --> 33:50.331
communities that are trying to adopt it and embrace, you know, the opportunity opportunities

33:50.363 --> 33:53.655
that go along with it. And so just a quick little bit

33:54.155 --> 33:57.350
about me to establish some credibility, you know, in what I'm talking about. I'm an

33:57.850 --> 34:00.160
Air Force veteran. I was an airfield manager in the Air Force. I spent a

34:00.660 --> 34:04.401
career planning, designing, and operating airports, heliports, and UAV infrastructure.

34:04.561 --> 34:07.677
And what we did differently in the military was,

34:07.774 --> 34:11.291
is that we brought aviation infrastructure and operations into areas

34:11.387 --> 34:14.311
that traditionally did not have aviation infrastructure and operations.

34:14.776 --> 34:19.042
And we strive to do that, you know, incredibly safely and efficiently so that our

34:19.170 --> 34:21.959
fellow service members had an opportunity to go back home to their families.

34:22.536 --> 34:25.676
And when I first retired from the military, you know, there wasn't a lot

34:26.176 --> 34:29.939
of, you know, direct applicability in doing that on the civilian side. But now

34:30.003 --> 34:33.913
with advanced mobility, there is a lot of correlation, you know, in that spirit

34:34.413 --> 34:38.032
of bringing new aviation aircraft infrastructure

34:38.160 --> 34:41.605
and operations into areas that didn't traditionally host these kinds of operations.

34:41.862 --> 34:45.363
Before, and then understanding and respecting the nuances,

34:45.796 --> 34:49.473
the concerns, and navigating that. And upon military retirement,

34:49.553 --> 34:52.492
I returned here back home where I was born and raised in Utah,

34:53.182 --> 34:56.907
got a degree in city and metropolitan planning, and have since worked to integrate

34:57.421 --> 35:00.905
aviation better into communities so that aviation can be a better industry in

35:01.405 --> 35:05.225
serving communities they serve. Because I am passionate about, you know, aviation because it gave

35:05.725 --> 35:08.338
me an opportunity that I otherwise wouldn't have had had to build a career,

35:08.354 --> 35:11.883
raise a family, and have something to support me. And that's,

35:11.979 --> 35:15.556
you know, why I'm passionate to be here and very grateful for this opportunity

35:15.572 --> 35:18.620
here. So, what is advancing mobility?

35:19.646 --> 35:23.336
I like to break it down into 3 general categories for people to understand because

35:23.836 --> 35:27.249
it's not just a flying car, it's not flying air taxis, it's not even

35:27.361 --> 35:30.730
the electric vertical takeoff and aircraft that you see on the

35:31.230 --> 35:35.365
front pages and the headlines that you see. Advancing mobility is typically characterized by

35:35.530 --> 35:38.959
3 evolving technologies, and it could be a mix of those

35:39.055 --> 35:42.055
3 technologies. The first one is new propulsion systems.

35:42.921 --> 35:45.937
The ones that's leading the race right now is electric propulsion systems.

35:46.435 --> 35:49.819
Just like every other mode of transportation, aviation is

35:50.319 --> 35:53.798
embracing electric technologies. And just like every other mode of transportation,

35:53.894 --> 35:57.263
we're also struggling with the issues that surface modes struggled

35:57.763 --> 36:01.514
with— range anxieties, where's the infrastructure, how do we recharge, where are we going

36:02.014 --> 36:05.381
to recharge. new firefighting capabilities, responding to instances and things

36:05.881 --> 36:08.172
of that nature. And so we're learning from the ground and we're, you know,

36:08.252 --> 36:12.183
having a new— enjoying new lines of communications from other modes

36:12.683 --> 36:16.049
of transportation and cross-pollinating new ideas on how we're addressing these

36:16.549 --> 36:20.140
challenges as we're going forward. And we're also learning about hybrid

36:20.640 --> 36:24.023
technologies, extending ranges through those capabilities and whatnot.

36:24.456 --> 36:27.713
And even hydrogen is starting to break into some conversations both in the air

36:28.186 --> 36:31.034
as a part of the aircraft systems, but also on the ground as part of

36:31.534 --> 36:35.365
the infrastructure and how we manage and charge aircraft and do other things.

36:36.200 --> 36:39.521
The second bucket that characterizes advanced air mobility is automation

36:39.537 --> 36:42.986
and autonomy. And this is something that is scary for a lot of people when

36:43.486 --> 36:47.238
you think about aircraft moving through the air, but aviation has embraced

36:47.738 --> 36:51.345
autonomy going back to the late '60s and into the '70s when we had

36:51.845 --> 36:55.707
a string of midair collisions that could have been avoided were caused by human error

36:56.207 --> 36:59.993
from pilots being oversaturated with tasks during difficult phases of flight. We introduced

37:00.493 --> 37:02.290
autonomy through autopilot,

37:04.680 --> 37:08.093
ground traffic avoidance systems, traffic collision avoidance

37:08.158 --> 37:12.376
systems, and those, you know, when we put those in the right places, it reduced

37:12.521 --> 37:16.206
the occurrence of those types of collisions and, you know, eventually made

37:16.238 --> 37:19.729
air traffic and airline travel the safest mode of transportation in the

37:19.752 --> 37:23.882
world within the United States. And so we've been incrementally adding autonomy

37:24.379 --> 37:27.824
to different parts of aviation, you know, since the '60s and '70s

37:28.324 --> 37:31.142
to today, where if you're flying on an airline today,

37:31.750 --> 37:35.196
as much as, you know, 95 to 98% of that flight is flown completely

37:35.696 --> 37:39.106
autonomous. You know, it's typically just the landing and the takeoff, you know, where the

37:39.606 --> 37:43.273
pilot, you know, has the controls. Everything else is done, you know, through waypoints,

37:43.289 --> 37:47.022
computers, and everything else to make sure that

37:47.522 --> 37:51.335
that we're doing things safely and whatnot. The 4th category is aerodynamics.

37:51.383 --> 37:54.653
And so you can see from this middle frame, the top picture in

37:55.153 --> 37:58.565
the middle frame, through advanced manufacturing, composite materials,

37:58.757 --> 38:02.733
and just AI and computers, we're understanding

38:02.845 --> 38:06.259
different ways that we can put aircraft together. We're looking at biomimicry,

38:06.436 --> 38:10.924
looking at how nature has done this, and we're putting aircraft

38:11.424 --> 38:14.392
together in different ways where we're understanding, you know, how aircraft move through the air

38:14.892 --> 38:18.261
more efficiently. And even if we're not, you know, putting new electric propulsion systems on

38:18.761 --> 38:22.018
the aircraft, we're achieving new levels of efficiency that we haven't seen before.

38:22.420 --> 38:25.872
And so when you put all this together, you know, you have different expressions of

38:26.372 --> 38:29.629
aircraft, different expressions of technology, and they all qualify under this

38:30.129 --> 38:33.756
umbrella that is advancing mobility. And you might see that in drone technologies, you might

38:34.256 --> 38:38.942
see that in short takeoff and landing technologies. Sometimes you might see it

38:39.442 --> 38:40.428
an aircraft that you might— it might look just like an aircraft that you've been

38:40.928 --> 38:44.489
seeing for the last 20 years, but you just don't know it because they've retrofitted

38:44.989 --> 38:48.023
something under the hood that is an electric propulsion retrofit

38:48.088 --> 38:52.115
or an autonomous systems retrofit, but it still qualifies as advanced air mobility.

38:54.008 --> 38:57.701
So we have different missions and use cases that are coming out of this that

38:57.959 --> 39:02.153
aren't necessarily new, but they're taking on new life because the technology has allowed

39:02.653 --> 39:06.267
us to bring these back up. And so regional air mobility has been something that

39:06.767 --> 39:10.968
the aviation industry has been trying to gain

39:11.468 --> 39:15.620
popularity, probably going back, you know, since the '50s and '60s. More recently,

39:15.636 --> 39:18.459
back in the 2000s, was something that we called the very light jet movement.

39:19.566 --> 39:22.775
But noise and emissions, you know, kind of killed that. And now that we got

39:23.275 --> 39:26.833
electric systems, you know, those noise and emissions

39:27.010 --> 39:30.458
and things of that nature aren't as big of an issue. And so now regional

39:30.958 --> 39:34.882
mobility is gathering more momentum. And regional mobility basically is just those trips

39:35.382 --> 39:39.098
that we don't really see happening within aviation today, which is really those

39:39.275 --> 39:42.160
in-state trips. Why don't we have trips from Salt Lake City to Moab?

39:42.561 --> 39:45.783
Well, it's too short for the airlines to really tackle, and it's

39:45.847 --> 39:49.678
too long for, you know, say a small helicopter or a small, I guess,

39:49.694 --> 39:52.580
Cessna to tackle. You know, and so those are the kinds of trips that we're

39:53.080 --> 39:57.376
starting to see now made possible by regional mobility. So getting

39:57.392 --> 40:00.443
out to the oil fields through Vernal, you know, that's a use case that

40:00.943 --> 40:04.216
when I— during my time at UDOT was very, very popular and

40:04.716 --> 40:07.187
we were trying to make happen. We just couldn't find an operator or an aircraft

40:07.687 --> 40:10.976
that could do that efficiently. Now we're starting to see a number of aircraft that

40:11.476 --> 40:14.830
are emerging under this that can make that happen. Same thing with the Moab,

40:14.894 --> 40:18.009
and there's a number of other use cases— Jackson,

40:18.137 --> 40:21.861
Sun Valley. All those now are becoming

40:21.877 --> 40:25.261
more possible with this. Urban air mobility has been attempted

40:25.761 --> 40:28.517
a number of different times throughout the history of aviation, and again,

40:28.549 --> 40:32.205
it's been stymied, you know, because of the impacts

40:32.318 --> 40:36.166
of noise, pollution, and things of that nature. And again, because of

40:36.666 --> 40:40.079
electric propulsion technologies, we're reducing the noise impact,

40:40.095 --> 40:43.447
we're reducing the emissions impact, and so urban air mobility is now a feasible

40:43.527 --> 40:46.719
technology. And that's the more shorter city-to-city

40:47.316 --> 40:51.111
Intra-city, you know, if you're in a very large city, probably less

40:51.223 --> 40:55.166
than, you know, 150 miles is really what we're looking at. And so Ogden to

40:55.666 --> 40:58.581
Provo, Eagle Mountain to Salt Lake City,

40:58.837 --> 41:02.957
you know, that is an urban air mobility trip to try and provide

41:03.457 --> 41:06.612
another option out there. And then we got the uncrewed or unmanned

41:07.112 --> 41:10.715
aerial systems, which is the drones. Drones started out, you know,

41:10.731 --> 41:13.777
as a hobby, and a lot of times we characterize that, you know,

41:13.841 --> 41:17.261
as a hobby, and it becomes annoying because it's a hobby, but we're now starting

41:17.293 --> 41:21.098
to do remote dirty and dangerous jobs with drones. We're gaining

41:21.146 --> 41:24.358
a lot of efficiency out of that, and it's becoming a real tool that complements,

41:24.406 --> 41:29.464
you know, a lot of different sectors to include rail inspection, power line inspection,

41:29.720 --> 41:33.253
avalanche control, wildfire control, and it's just getting a

41:33.753 --> 41:37.010
lot of popularity. You know, we probably haven't even gotten half of the use cases

41:37.042 --> 41:39.708
that we're going to see in the next 20 years, you know, with different drone

41:39.792 --> 41:43.258
technologies here. And so all of those, again, those use cases,

41:43.323 --> 41:46.565
those utilities are all emerging under the advanced air mobility umbrella.

41:48.393 --> 41:51.680
And we can see here some of the characteristics of what regional mobility

41:51.761 --> 41:55.112
is along with an example aircraft here from Heart Aerospace.

41:56.091 --> 41:59.112
And again, it's largely what we talked about, you know, 125 miles,

41:59.626 --> 42:03.000
745 miles. Those larger miles, you know,

42:03.884 --> 42:06.613
that's the far end of the capability of the aircraft. I know a lot of

42:07.113 --> 42:09.668
the operators now are trying to find a sweet spot in the middle to where,

42:09.732 --> 42:12.486
you know, we don't have to, you know, go visiting the bathroom a whole lot

42:12.986 --> 42:16.140
of time. There are some new nuances that are popping up within this that's influencing

42:16.253 --> 42:19.778
some of the route designs and things of that nature, but the point being is

42:19.810 --> 42:23.198
that the technology is starting to meet some of the aspirations

42:23.698 --> 42:28.168
that we've had for some time. eVTOL characteristics,

42:28.297 --> 42:32.077
it's a— this is really what got the industry started. We owe a debt

42:32.577 --> 42:35.255
of gratitude to eVTOL even though we might might not see these technologies in Utah

42:35.335 --> 42:38.783
for quite some time. You know, this did capture the imagination

42:39.283 --> 42:42.456
of a lot of the investors, a lot of technologists, and that helped, you know,

42:42.472 --> 42:45.631
really lift up the entire industry and all the other use cases that we will

42:45.663 --> 42:49.063
see in Utah and move us along and get

42:49.563 --> 42:53.217
the technology to this point. Now, why might

42:53.717 --> 42:57.226
I see eVTOL, you know, real soon here in Utah? Well, there's a

42:57.726 --> 43:00.595
lot of different trade-offs, you know, with when we're trying to lift aircraft or heavy

43:01.095 --> 43:04.850
vehicles off the ground vertically. You know, and those are influenced by different

43:05.350 --> 43:08.816
factors to include the environment. You know, here in Utah, we live in a high

43:09.025 --> 43:12.477
altitude where the air is thinner. That air gets thinner when it gets hotter.

43:12.557 --> 43:16.411
That air gets thinner when it's more humid. And, you know,

43:16.973 --> 43:20.329
the aircraft manufacturers are still working on how to handle that, you know,

43:20.345 --> 43:23.637
in different climates. And so we're going to see these technologies propagate first

43:24.137 --> 43:27.940
in coastal areas. Warm climates and things of that nature. And as they refine

43:27.956 --> 43:31.552
the battery technology, they refine the other things that are maybe limiting,

43:31.729 --> 43:34.796
you know, in more challenging environments,

43:35.037 --> 43:37.975
then we'll start seeing that emerge in other use cases,

43:38.007 --> 43:41.508
probably through, you know, southern Utah. We're seeing some use cases looking

43:41.540 --> 43:44.012
at St. George to Las Vegas and stuff like that. And then it's going to

43:44.512 --> 43:48.171
propagate further north. And hopefully we'll start seeing that maybe in the 2030

43:48.187 --> 43:50.499
timeframe, hopefully before the Olympics.

43:54.251 --> 43:57.890
And we can see some of the leading aircraft manufacturers here that are leading

43:57.906 --> 44:01.746
the eVTOL race currently today. Eve Air Mobility is

44:01.794 --> 44:04.991
a great company. They're led by a legacy aircraft manufacturer

44:05.491 --> 44:09.747
called Embraer. They make private jets today. They got, you know, decades of experience

44:10.247 --> 44:14.182
building jets, certifying jets, and delivering jets. Joby Aviation,

44:14.230 --> 44:17.427
a relative newcomer in the area, but they got backing by Toyota. Toyota,

44:18.069 --> 44:22.003
looking at, you know, gaining efficiencies through Toyota's manufacturing

44:22.503 --> 44:25.600
processes. Beta Technologies, great company.

44:26.419 --> 44:29.566
FutureFlight Global actually just put in an order for 10 Beta aircraft

44:30.066 --> 44:33.018
that we hope to operate also within Utah in the future sometime.

44:33.644 --> 44:36.952
And Archer Aircraft is looking at operating for the 2028 Olympics

44:37.452 --> 44:40.677
down in Los Angeles, and hopefully we'll see and gain some lessons learned

44:40.709 --> 44:43.935
there that we might apply to Utah. in the coming years.

44:46.840 --> 44:50.606
And so, you know, I think a lot of people, you know, throughout the growth

44:51.106 --> 44:54.534
of this industry have seen this and say, oh, flying cars, we've been promised this

44:55.034 --> 44:57.119
for such a long time, and, you know, expected it just to kind of fade

44:57.619 --> 45:00.925
off into the distance. You know, we've seen strong growth. Yes, we have seen some

45:01.425 --> 45:04.684
contraction, and that's to be expected in any development of technology,

45:06.008 --> 45:09.274
but, you know, the strong companies have stayed strong. And they're

45:09.774 --> 45:13.900
continuing to push along. They're continuing to gain investment. They're continuing to consolidate,

45:13.916 --> 45:17.706
you know, in the right places. And, you know, we're still seeing certification

45:18.206 --> 45:22.043
timelines that are, you know, continuing to get closer and closer. The latest estimates

45:22.124 --> 45:25.657
for the beta aircraft, which is a conventional takeoff and landing

45:26.157 --> 45:29.802
aircraft, meaning it's going to take off from a runway on the wing, is 4th

45:29.834 --> 45:34.235
quarter of 2027, possibly 1st quarter of 2028.

45:34.684 --> 45:38.153
for the eVTOLs is looking into 2028. I think they're expecting,

45:38.249 --> 45:40.657
hoping to have it by the Olympics in the summertime,

45:41.540 --> 45:44.928
and we'll see it propagate from there. And, you know,

45:45.394 --> 45:49.279
that's a— it's getting pretty real. And with the EIPP, which Utah is

45:49.779 --> 45:53.726
a part of, the whole purpose of this program is for the manufacturers

45:53.775 --> 45:57.291
to provide data to the FAA so

45:57.355 --> 46:01.363
that we can safely certify these aircraft in a safer and

46:01.558 --> 46:03.473
more faster manner.

46:08.386 --> 46:10.974
And, you know, we can see how much we, you know, I guess the industry

46:10.990 --> 46:13.967
has embraced there. So the airline industry has embraced, you know, we have a lot

46:14.467 --> 46:17.719
of government, almost all states have engaged in some way,

46:17.799 --> 46:21.022
shape, or form in the advanced air mobility industry. Most states have done some kind

46:21.522 --> 46:24.707
of economic impact study. FutureFlight Global is working, you know,

46:24.723 --> 46:28.122
in Michigan, in Florida, in Utah and Arizona. You know,

46:28.202 --> 46:31.028
there's just a lot of different areas that are really excited about this.

46:31.349 --> 46:34.480
Tennessee's got an economic impact study that's a pretty novel one that's

46:34.980 --> 46:39.056
coming out probably next year. Medical logistics, just all different industries

46:39.072 --> 46:41.995
are starting to look at how they can embrace and utilize this technology because,

46:42.589 --> 46:46.330
again, you know, with the efficiencies that we're gaining from this, the hope is,

46:46.940 --> 46:50.103
is that we're going to make aviation more accessible to more uses.

46:50.617 --> 46:54.533
And what does that mean? Aviation has historically been

46:55.033 --> 46:58.399
there to serve high-value assets and people.

46:58.945 --> 47:02.330
That's it. CEOs, the people you see chartering private jets

47:02.830 --> 47:06.935
or private helicopters flying across the sky, things of that nature, that's what

47:07.047 --> 47:10.432
aviation has done. I've always said that

47:10.932 --> 47:14.251
that's not really going to change into the future. What is going to change is

47:14.283 --> 47:18.150
the calculus around how we define what qualifies as high-value and time critical.

47:18.681 --> 47:22.280
And the greatest example I have of that, you know, was pre-COVID and post-COVID,

47:22.714 --> 47:25.944
or I should, you know, on the onset of COVID and post-COVID. In the onset

47:26.444 --> 47:30.395
of COVID and when I— during my time at Utah, we saw $50

47:30.813 --> 47:34.348
mining equipment, you know, that was being moved via private aircraft because

47:34.848 --> 47:38.622
of the bottlenecks at the— on the ground with the trucking at

47:39.122 --> 47:42.318
the borders. You know, these $50 mining equipment was being moved at the cost of

47:42.350 --> 47:45.522
around $60,000. $60,000 via private air because they needed that

47:46.022 --> 47:48.923
equipment faster than the surface networks can get it there.

47:49.646 --> 47:51.640
That's high-value, time-critical.

47:53.264 --> 47:55.804
We get to the end of COVID as it was wrapping up, and we had

47:57.591 --> 48:01.480
Zipline and DroneUp delivering drone delivery here in northern Utah out

48:01.980 --> 48:05.998
at Herriman. The number one drone delivery asset that was being delivered from Walmart

48:06.498 --> 48:11.822
was cookies and cream ice cream and paper towels. And so that became time-critical

48:12.322 --> 48:14.512
and high value, you know, in that context, you know. And so you look at

48:14.561 --> 48:17.816
those ranges of extremes and what is in the middle that we're missing.

48:18.249 --> 48:21.247
And I think that's what, you know, is exciting about how we can define for

48:21.295 --> 48:24.390
Eagle Mountain City, you know, what is in that middle that you want to define,

48:24.487 --> 48:27.148
you know, as high value, time critical, that you want to give access to your

48:27.648 --> 48:29.955
residents to. And I think all that now is possible here.

48:30.837 --> 48:34.316
And so with that, we can move into the market analysis

48:34.990 --> 48:38.052
and explore, you know, what the nuts and bolts are starting tell us here.

48:38.373 --> 48:41.754
And so, you know, with any market analysis here,

48:41.882 --> 48:45.007
you know, I know you didn't hire a consultant to come in, you know,

48:45.103 --> 48:48.292
and tell you that you're a small growing community, you know, and those kinds of

48:48.792 --> 48:52.298
things, but that's important to you around the context here. Because if

48:52.798 --> 48:56.000
we came in here and said you're a small growing community and that equates to

48:56.500 --> 48:59.606
that there's not a whole lot of aviation demand today, I think a lot of

49:00.106 --> 49:02.907
places would have said, all right, well, let's come back in 20 years and measure

49:02.971 --> 49:06.149
again and see what that means, and maybe we'll reapproach this. Well,

49:06.181 --> 49:08.509
in 20 years, you're going to run out of space, you're going to run out

49:09.009 --> 49:11.143
of land, you're going to have a whole different nature, you know, of what the

49:11.643 --> 49:14.884
community is going to look like. And I think what we want to say

49:15.012 --> 49:18.705
is, is in 20 years, the decisions that you make today are going

49:19.205 --> 49:22.318
to define what Eagle Mountain looks like. And if we want to

49:22.818 --> 49:26.011
embrace, you know, the emerging technologies, you know, that's going to define not just the

49:26.075 --> 49:29.250
transportation options that are available, but the jobs, you know, that want to be available

49:29.750 --> 49:33.350
in this community, the decisions that we're making today is going to paint that

49:33.850 --> 49:36.950
picture of what that's going to look like in 20 years and whether or not

49:37.450 --> 49:40.710
aviation or advanced air mobility— and eventually we're going to drop advanced from that because

49:41.210 --> 49:42.970
it's just going to be the way we're doing things. You know, if we want

49:43.470 --> 49:46.610
aviation to be a part of that dynamic, you know, those— if that's what's going

49:47.110 --> 49:49.470
to keep the kids in the community, that's something that a lot of communities struggle

49:49.970 --> 49:53.210
with. You know, that's the decisions that we're making today. And I think that's what

49:53.710 --> 49:54.090
we're trying to grow here.

49:57.897 --> 50:01.588
So the main thing here, again, you know, I know, you know,

50:01.830 --> 50:05.573
you already know that you're a small community and that you're growing, but one

50:06.073 --> 50:09.558
of the things I want to, you know, highlight here as a takeaway, there have

50:09.591 --> 50:12.754
been some areas within this industry over the last 5 years where the

50:12.819 --> 50:16.289
industry goes out looking for places to manufacture aircraft or looks for

50:16.789 --> 50:20.291
places to expand growth for other sectors of the industry, meaning that it

50:20.307 --> 50:23.715
wants to add jobs to community. They're looking for a place to grow.

50:24.100 --> 50:27.673
And typically the communities that are put in the race

50:27.705 --> 50:31.087
for trying to attract this, it turns into a race for the bottom. You know,

50:31.167 --> 50:33.683
they're just trying to figure out how they can get the most incentives in there,

50:33.891 --> 50:37.481
and the industry plays that game to pit communities against each other to, you know,

50:37.497 --> 50:40.990
get that race to the bottom. And largely, you know, what it comes

50:41.006 --> 50:44.660
down to is who has the best trained workforce,

50:45.029 --> 50:48.220
and when that doesn't work out, who has the best incentives. incentive package. And what

50:48.720 --> 50:52.539
I've— we really liked about here is Eagle Mountain has an awesome workforce,

50:53.133 --> 50:56.682
a very skilled workforce, but they're leaving. You know, every single

50:57.182 --> 51:00.600
morning they're leaving Eagle Mountain and going out to Salt Lake City, going out to

51:01.100 --> 51:05.465
Lehi, and things of that nature. And it's putting all this congestion and pressure

51:05.642 --> 51:08.532
on your surface transportation corridors, you know,

51:08.612 --> 51:11.920
leading up to, you know, heavy commute times and so

51:12.420 --> 51:16.094
forth. Thank you. And so, when we look at aviation and we look at advanced

51:16.594 --> 51:19.787
air mobility and everything else around it, oftentimes we

51:20.287 --> 51:23.529
get stuck into thinking that aviation is just a mode of transportation.

51:23.962 --> 51:27.366
It's a way to get from point A to point B. And we want

51:27.866 --> 51:31.332
to expand the aperture on the opportunities around aviation beyond just

51:31.832 --> 51:35.651
a mode of transportation and also into how does aviation serve as

51:35.683 --> 51:39.182
an economic development opportunity. A land development opportunity,

51:39.599 --> 51:43.464
a workforce development opportunity. And so then we start looking at, as Steve was

51:43.964 --> 51:47.265
saying, you know, what does this look like from an AAM hub perspective and how

51:47.313 --> 51:51.130
do we anchor jobs? You know, and so if we have this workforce that's leaving,

51:51.707 --> 51:54.979
what can we build to keep those jobs within the community?

51:55.636 --> 51:58.780
You know, look at those workforces. You know, we got— we did a NAICS code

51:59.280 --> 52:03.190
inventory. You know, if we got highly, you know, technical professional science

52:03.494 --> 52:06.849
jobs, How do we attract those employers to keep them in the community

52:07.186 --> 52:10.733
and link them up with just-in-time delivery opportunities, you know, that might be

52:11.233 --> 52:15.772
paired, you know, with drone logistics in the future, or maybe

52:15.933 --> 52:19.319
aircraft maintenance opportunities in the future? Eventually,

52:19.400 --> 52:21.968
we're going to, you know, get to the point to where we need pilots,

52:22.192 --> 52:25.643
or we need air traffic controllers, or we need maintainers, you know, on this.

52:26.044 --> 52:28.532
You know, all that can be a part of the workforce development, you know,

52:28.548 --> 52:31.486
that we're building here. here, and so on.

52:32.482 --> 52:35.084
And so these are the things that we want to plant the seeds for today

52:36.015 --> 52:39.628
because, again, the decisions that we're making today and how we think

52:40.128 --> 52:43.499
about this stuff today is really what's going to influence what Eagle Mountain looks like

52:43.999 --> 52:47.000
in 20 years. And if we're just thinking about, hey, we want a fancy flying

52:47.500 --> 52:50.565
car that's going to get 4 people from downtown Eagle Mountain to

52:51.031 --> 52:53.889
the point of the mountain 20 minutes away,

52:54.804 --> 52:57.679
I think we're thinking a little bit short-sighted on this. You know, I think that

52:58.179 --> 53:00.746
is an option, you know, that we definitely want to keep open to. But I

53:01.246 --> 53:04.086
think the more exciting option of this is how do we keep those thousands of

53:04.102 --> 53:07.634
people that are, you know, getting on that road and heading out of town and

53:08.134 --> 53:11.327
keep them inside of Eagle Mountain, keep that tax base inside of Eagle Mountain,

53:11.343 --> 53:14.779
keep those revenues inside of Eagle Mountain, provide more opportunities, you know,

53:14.795 --> 53:18.296
those secondary and tertiary economic impacts, and grow that from within.

53:22.534 --> 53:26.404
And that's really, you know, what we're starting to really see the opportunities

53:26.517 --> 53:30.740
emerging around here. And it's

53:30.772 --> 53:34.047
really inspiring, you know, to see, you know, what you have here and the fact

53:34.547 --> 53:37.082
that your city leadership has identified this opportunity.

53:37.676 --> 53:41.176
There seems to be, you know, a lot of political support, the state support,

53:41.208 --> 53:44.805
you know, in Utah. Everything's coming together here, you know, as this, you know,

53:44.821 --> 53:49.638
perfect opportunity to start growing and seeing what this looks like to take

53:50.138 --> 53:52.508
take advantage not just of the opportunities you got here, but, you know, recognize the

53:53.008 --> 53:56.069
challenges and bring some unique solutions. And one of the things that the

53:56.569 --> 53:59.566
state of Utah has done is created this Advancing Mobility Policy Toolkit.

54:00.288 --> 54:03.560
And how they've positioned this toolkit is not as

54:03.978 --> 54:07.667
you should look at advancing mobility as a solution to all your problems. It's you

54:08.167 --> 54:11.325
should look at advancing mobility as a complement to what you're already doing to solve

54:11.357 --> 54:15.079
your problems. And so if Eagle Mountain City is trying to you

54:15.579 --> 54:19.478
know, create new college and workforce development opportunities by attracting UVU or

54:19.978 --> 54:23.572
USU or another college, you know, out here. How can aviation complement that?

54:24.199 --> 54:27.394
Um, and we've done this, you know, in other jurisdictions where we've asked the hard

54:27.426 --> 54:30.894
questions of the industry and saying, hey, we have a housing shortage problem

54:30.942 --> 54:34.218
at this income level, you know, how are you going to solve this? And of

54:34.250 --> 54:36.739
course, you know, the industry comes back and it's like, we have no idea,

54:36.899 --> 54:40.928
like we weren't expecting to solve this problem. They'll go back and and

54:41.428 --> 54:44.533
be frustrated a little bit, but at least gets them thinking about it. And more

54:44.581 --> 54:47.850
times than not, they'll come back with some answers. There's some responses.

54:48.186 --> 54:51.263
Sometimes they're not good responses, but we at least got them thinking about it.

54:51.327 --> 54:55.092
And now we're starting to think about solving problems out there that we

54:55.592 --> 54:59.578
actually have. And this is what we're looking at here in terms of the

55:00.078 --> 55:02.927
problems that Eagle Mountain has is, hey, we got a large workforce that's leaving the

55:03.427 --> 55:06.372
city. How do we keep them in the city? What are the options that we

55:06.872 --> 55:09.854
have beyond just putting them on an air taxi and shipping them out? How do

55:10.354 --> 55:14.145
we do this together? The land use controls, the planning, the economic development,

55:14.579 --> 55:17.857
the transportation planning, the whole 9 yards can go

55:17.873 --> 55:18.178
into this.

55:21.716 --> 55:24.754
Then we can start looking at, all right, now that we got the seeds planted,

55:25.108 --> 55:28.468
what does this look like from an aviation perspective? And so for this, we looked

55:28.968 --> 55:31.615
at the regional airports that exist around here— South Valley Airport,

55:31.993 --> 55:35.269
Spanish Fork Airport, Provo Airport. And looked

55:35.769 --> 55:39.215
at the typical corporate jet operations and just tried

55:39.715 --> 55:42.049
to look at a proxy of saying, let's look at Eagle Mountain in the future

55:42.549 --> 55:46.038
when we have those corporate employers, when we have the folks

55:48.191 --> 55:50.829
that are providing those jobs, where are they going?

55:51.447 --> 55:55.096
Why would they locate here and where would they go to? What economies are

55:55.596 --> 55:58.744
they connecting to? And so we found a heavy connection to Las Vegas

55:58.970 --> 56:02.513
and Henderson, Nevada. And that could be for recreation,

56:02.561 --> 56:05.189
it could be for tourism, it could be for second homes, you know. But the

56:05.689 --> 56:09.243
point being, now you have connected economies, you know, and there's synergies there

56:09.275 --> 56:11.695
that can be built out from that. You know, the same with St. George.

56:11.743 --> 56:15.284
You know, we learned just the other day that there's people that

56:15.784 --> 56:18.922
live in Ogden that are bypassing the Salt Lake City airport to drive to Provo

56:19.434 --> 56:22.799
to get, you know, on a flight to Phoenix to go to their second homes

56:23.299 --> 56:26.549
there because they don't want to deal with the hassles at Salt Lake City.

56:27.262 --> 56:31.292
So there's an opportunity there. What can we do with that opportunity? The same

56:31.309 --> 56:34.594
thing with Orange County, the Denver area. And so with

56:35.094 --> 56:38.247
this data that we got from the state, what can we do to start saying

56:38.584 --> 56:42.350
in the future we're connected to these economies already within this

56:42.850 --> 56:46.083
region here in Utah County? What can we do now to start building

56:46.131 --> 56:49.993
on that today to make that connection more robust

56:50.493 --> 56:54.695
into the future? What can we do to Make Eagle Mountain better connected

56:55.195 --> 56:58.912
to Las Vegas and Henderson, Nevada to make that economic connection more meaningful?

56:59.316 --> 57:02.687
Is there a job connection? Is there a recreation connection? Is there

57:02.704 --> 57:06.180
something else that we can be doing here if we had that regional mobility where

57:06.680 --> 57:10.191
the seats are now comparable or even better than getting on an airline

57:10.789 --> 57:12.650
and have that access right here within the community?

57:15.279 --> 57:18.474
I'll pass it over to Steve now for the drive time. Great,

57:18.539 --> 57:22.153
thanks. So what are the benefits an aviation

57:22.169 --> 57:25.057
hub could provide for Eagle Mountain City?

57:26.138 --> 57:29.973
We, as part of our study, we did a drive time

57:30.022 --> 57:34.111
analysis, and this particular graphic shows how

57:34.611 --> 57:38.036
long it takes to go from Eagle Mountain to some of the other aviation

57:38.069 --> 57:39.672
facilities within the region.

57:41.916 --> 57:44.857
You've got West Desert Airport, which is pretty close.

57:45.986 --> 57:50.073
But your next closest airport is Provo Airport, and it takes somewhere between 40

57:50.089 --> 57:54.103
and 45 minutes to access that airport

57:54.603 --> 57:58.663
for aviation purposes. When we look at the catchment areas for

57:58.743 --> 58:02.163
each of these airports, Eagle Mountain kind of sits in

58:02.195 --> 58:06.033
the middle where you're served by both

58:06.627 --> 58:09.597
for aviation needs at Tooele Valley Airport,

58:09.613 --> 58:13.132
Salt Lake City, South Valley Regional, Provo Airport,

58:13.678 --> 58:17.326
and so all of these airports within the region

58:17.826 --> 58:21.088
are serving residents of Eagle Mountain City. As you

58:21.588 --> 58:25.372
can see on this graphic, there's an opportunity for Eagle Mountain City to capture

58:27.031 --> 58:30.140
the market that relies on these other airports,

58:31.060 --> 58:34.723
as well as aviation needs within the

58:34.868 --> 58:38.776
region. When we look at the potential user groups within

58:39.276 --> 58:43.223
the area. It can serve the multitude of advanced

58:43.723 --> 58:47.936
air mobility vehicle types. It can help serve

58:48.049 --> 58:51.938
military operations for training, and it can draw

58:51.986 --> 58:56.230
corporations into the community and serve for their corporate

58:56.263 --> 58:59.222
location, for their aviation hangars, and so forth.

59:01.960 --> 59:05.743
The drive— when we look at the drive time analysis

59:06.243 --> 59:09.638
again, From Eagle Mountain City, there's opportunities to serve

59:11.098 --> 59:15.574
various airports within the region as well as warehouses,

59:16.392 --> 59:19.665
large warehouses, hospitals, all within

59:20.643 --> 59:24.718
short distance from from Eagle Mountain City. So there's opportunities

59:25.215 --> 59:28.584
where aviation users would want to provide

59:29.084 --> 59:32.755
access to these facilities. Similarly, there's need.

59:32.920 --> 59:36.350
for military, alternative military locations

59:36.366 --> 59:39.640
for practice and training and so forth. So there's an opportunity

59:40.250 --> 59:43.524
to support those needs as well. One of the

59:43.588 --> 59:47.745
primary opportunities is going to be for corporate

59:47.777 --> 59:51.083
facilities. So larger corporations that

59:51.211 --> 59:54.790
don't want to have to drive up to Salt Lake City to access

59:54.902 --> 59:58.594
their corporate aircraft could potentially locate their corporate

59:59.094 --> 01:00:02.613
aircraft in Eagle Mountain City where it's more accessible, easier to

01:00:03.113 --> 01:00:06.871
get to, and so forth. So what are the requirements for

01:00:07.337 --> 01:00:10.631
developing an aviation hub? There's a number of

01:00:10.711 --> 01:00:14.134
different types of requirements depending on the type of facility

01:00:14.150 --> 01:00:18.324
that you want to develop. What I'd like to do is talk about the general

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:22.291
overall requirements for aviation facilities, then talk about some

01:00:22.323 --> 01:00:25.903
of the specifics for advanced air mobility

01:00:26.018 --> 01:00:29.255
facilities. Talk about the difference in what might

01:00:29.755 --> 01:00:33.021
be needed for military aviation, and then also some of

01:00:33.521 --> 01:00:35.713
the differences that would be required for general aviation.

01:00:36.530 --> 01:00:41.081
So, in general, an airport facility requires

01:00:41.337 --> 01:00:45.279
a runway where the aircraft land and depart.

01:00:46.625 --> 01:00:49.846
In addition, it needs an adjacent parallel

01:00:50.346 --> 01:00:54.062
taxiway that allows aircraft to access the runway ends

01:00:54.094 --> 01:00:57.596
for their arrival and departure. The airport requires

01:00:57.869 --> 01:01:01.483
navaids to help guide the aircraft that would be located on the

01:01:01.983 --> 01:01:06.108
ground. It requires generally an apron area

01:01:06.446 --> 01:01:09.979
that is approximately 1.5 times the

01:01:10.060 --> 01:01:13.368
area of the hangars that would be supported at an airport.

01:01:14.011 --> 01:01:17.962
It requires vehicle access to the facility and then some

01:01:18.026 --> 01:01:21.624
type of security to Keep people off

01:01:22.124 --> 01:01:25.558
the airport that are not authorized to be on the airport. When we

01:01:26.058 --> 01:01:30.842
look at the requirements for a runway in particular, there's 3 primary protection

01:01:31.035 --> 01:01:34.761
areas that you'll want to provide for if

01:01:34.793 --> 01:01:38.567
you opt, or at some time opt to develop a runway.

01:01:38.969 --> 01:01:42.839
The first is the runway safety area. This is an area centered

01:01:43.339 --> 01:01:47.416
on the runway that nothing can penetrate or go into that area

01:01:47.481 --> 01:01:51.576
while the runway is active. In addition, there's a runway object-free

01:01:51.592 --> 01:01:55.028
area, which is a little broader than— and

01:01:55.108 --> 01:01:59.026
it's— all of these surfaces are centered on the runway centerline.

01:01:59.524 --> 01:02:03.779
But this is a larger area that allows for navigational

01:02:04.279 --> 01:02:07.937
facilities or facilities that are required by function to be within these

01:02:08.050 --> 01:02:11.518
areas. And this area extends beyond

01:02:12.018 --> 01:02:15.259
the runway by about 1,000 feet,

01:02:15.340 --> 01:02:19.241
which would be the requirement here at Eagle Mountain

01:02:19.741 --> 01:02:23.397
City. In addition, there's a runway protection zone,

01:02:23.429 --> 01:02:26.502
which is an area to protect people and property on the

01:02:26.568 --> 01:02:30.478
ground. This is a trapezoidal shape that varies.

01:02:31.886 --> 01:02:35.784
It's smaller as it is closer to the runway threshold,

01:02:35.848 --> 01:02:39.295
to the end of the runway, fans out as it gets further away.

01:02:41.806 --> 01:02:44.060
When we look at the requirements for a vertiport,

01:02:44.772 --> 01:02:48.034
They're much more reduced. You only need a

01:02:48.534 --> 01:02:51.556
pad for takeoff and approach.

01:02:53.278 --> 01:02:57.119
You need an apron area similar to the runway where the aircraft can

01:02:58.597 --> 01:03:02.407
be stationed when they're not operating. It would require

01:03:02.472 --> 01:03:06.461
some type of hangar or terminal facility that might include offices

01:03:06.961 --> 01:03:10.498
where corporations might want to have offices.

01:03:10.990 --> 01:03:14.383
space where they can lease while they're

01:03:14.431 --> 01:03:18.458
positioning or getting ready to fly their aircraft.

01:03:18.878 --> 01:03:22.316
And then I already mentioned the requirement for the apron.

01:03:25.119 --> 01:03:28.999
Some of the requirements for the vertiport are based on the type of aircraft

01:03:29.499 --> 01:03:33.153
that are flown. I mentioned already the takeoff and landing

01:03:33.249 --> 01:03:35.975
zone, which is where the aircraft are moving.

01:03:37.385 --> 01:03:41.801
The parking stands are additional areas to park

01:03:42.301 --> 01:03:45.494
any number of eVTOL aircraft while they're not in use,

01:03:45.815 --> 01:03:49.765
and then there are zones for airspace protection that are required

01:03:50.265 --> 01:03:53.329
and need to be defined for each of the eVTOL pads that would

01:03:53.426 --> 01:03:57.167
be developed. This is a general concept that

01:03:57.295 --> 01:04:00.699
would identify how you might configure each individual

01:04:00.892 --> 01:04:05.558
eVTOL pad. When we look at military operations,

01:04:05.784 --> 01:04:10.363
the requirement is probably for a runway of about 600

01:04:10.863 --> 01:04:14.155
to 2,000 feet. It all depends on the type of operations that

01:04:14.171 --> 01:04:18.335
the military would want to exercise.

01:04:18.851 --> 01:04:21.808
We assume that it would be for helicopter activities.

01:04:22.408 --> 01:04:25.666
Similar to the runway, although this runway would be shorter,

01:04:25.715 --> 01:04:28.252
it needs taxiway, it needs security,

01:04:29.070 --> 01:04:32.617
uh, it needs apron area and a

01:04:32.777 --> 01:04:36.388
dedicated practice area where the aircraft would be able to leave the airport and

01:04:36.888 --> 01:04:40.640
then practice. When we look at the needs for corporate aircraft

01:04:41.140 --> 01:04:44.652
requirements, those requirements are probably the greatest of

01:04:45.152 --> 01:04:49.289
all. We look at the potential of a runway of about 6,000

01:04:49.789 --> 01:04:53.542
feet that would be required. This would be the required runway

01:04:54.042 --> 01:04:58.425
length to serve corporate aircraft in this region and at this elevation

01:04:59.003 --> 01:05:02.554
of the airport. In addition, it would need

01:05:03.054 --> 01:05:06.757
a fuel facility that would provide fuel for both corporate aircraft

01:05:07.257 --> 01:05:11.109
as well as low-lead fuel

01:05:11.142 --> 01:05:13.605
for small fixed-wing aircraft.

01:05:16.589 --> 01:05:20.270
Similar to the other facilities, the general requirements, it needs

01:05:20.598 --> 01:05:23.134
space for nav aids as well as apron area.

01:05:24.661 --> 01:05:27.957
When we look at the summary of all of these, as you think about

01:05:28.457 --> 01:05:32.000
how you might want to implement an aviation hub, you might

01:05:32.049 --> 01:05:35.321
want to begin small with an

01:05:35.370 --> 01:05:39.145
eVTOL facility and build

01:05:39.645 --> 01:05:43.908
on— depending on the users that would be located

01:05:44.408 --> 01:05:48.057
at the facility, you would be requiring additional

01:05:48.557 --> 01:05:51.827
facilities as the airport grows into the future.

01:05:54.904 --> 01:05:58.671
As we look at the next steps, we're in the process now of finalizing

01:05:58.929 --> 01:06:02.470
our report that would be submitted to

01:06:02.970 --> 01:06:06.395
this city within a couple of weeks.

01:06:06.477 --> 01:06:10.241
By the end of this month is our goal. So we're

01:06:10.257 --> 01:06:16.240
finalizing our findings and recommendations, and we're And

01:06:16.740 --> 01:06:19.412
with that, I'd like to entertain any questions you might have.

