01:15:45.200 --> 01:15:47.707
I call it the sounds of freedom. My son comes out and you can hear

01:15:48.207 --> 01:15:52.011
him on the range doing live fire and things of

01:15:52.511 --> 01:15:55.652
that nature. And I just, There comes a point

01:15:56.152 --> 01:15:59.620
where I start to say, What happens to the noise in the valley as

01:15:59.717 --> 01:16:03.187
we continually add more noise, especially things that

01:16:03.687 --> 01:16:06.855
are inherently noisy? They're not quiet. None of these

01:16:06.957 --> 01:16:10.272
things are. And so that's the first thing that I was thinking about.

01:16:10.867 --> 01:16:14.759
One of the comments that you made where we're fronting the money—that's extremely concerning to

01:16:15.259 --> 01:16:18.572
me because I'm not really in

01:16:18.604 --> 01:16:22.034
the business of giving money that we don't get a return

01:16:22.066 --> 01:16:25.862
on investment for quite some time or on an. And that actually leads

01:16:25.895 --> 01:16:29.484
to what Councilmember Clark said. You know, when Tyson left, that was

01:16:29.533 --> 01:16:32.734
a huge detriment to our community. This fails,

01:16:33.171 --> 01:16:36.586
we have to find some way to recover that immediately because we've put the

01:16:37.086 --> 01:16:40.035
investment into it. And what does that look like if it doesn't work?

01:16:41.687 --> 01:16:44.875
So those 2 things are maybe at the very forefront of my thought. I'm going

01:16:45.375 --> 01:16:48.339
to tell you, I don't love the area that's being contemplated at all.

01:16:48.728 --> 01:16:52.222
I've been pretty straightforward about that. I think if I was going

01:16:52.722 --> 01:16:54.936
to get on board with anything like this, I would want to move it south.

01:16:55.931 --> 01:16:58.822
So those are kind of some of the things that are banging around. I don't

01:16:59.322 --> 01:17:02.194
have a problem with the technologies. I think that we need to embrace some of

01:17:02.694 --> 01:17:06.192
those things. I think we need to try to remove

01:17:06.692 --> 01:17:09.596
as much of an impact as we can for the people who live here,

01:17:10.382 --> 01:17:13.578
because at the end of the day, what do they care if

01:17:14.078 --> 01:17:17.206
we're getting revenue into the city? Thank you. I mean, if we're being totally honest,

01:17:17.784 --> 01:17:20.065
do they care if we brought $30 million into the city?

01:17:20.996 --> 01:17:24.303
No, they care about the fact that they can't go outside and talk to their

01:17:24.336 --> 01:17:28.013
family during a barbecue because there's constant flights going

01:17:28.125 --> 01:17:31.577
over the top of their houses. And so I try to be sensitive to

01:17:32.077 --> 01:17:35.254
those types of things and what is reality. If we're talking about what is the

01:17:35.754 --> 01:17:38.144
quality of life, and that for me, that's one of the most important things of

01:17:38.644 --> 01:17:42.271
the decisions that I make in anything that we do in Eagle Mountain. Is in

01:17:42.319 --> 01:17:45.867
20 years, what did we do? Did our decisions make

01:17:46.367 --> 01:17:49.335
it better for them, or did we make decisions that they're like, "Who are the

01:17:49.835 --> 01:17:52.932
idiots that made these decisions 20 years ago? I don't want to live here anymore.

01:17:53.317 --> 01:17:56.929
I want to move to a place where there's peace again." So that's

01:17:57.429 --> 01:18:00.799
what's banging around in my head with all of these decisions about bringing an airport.

01:18:00.879 --> 01:18:04.074
I see all the economic benefits of it. There are a lot of

01:18:04.154 --> 01:18:07.237
ifs, however. Whenever you have startups.

01:18:08.911 --> 01:18:11.155
Startups succeed and startups fail.

01:18:12.343 --> 01:18:16.502
And when you're first in, in an industry, generally speaking,

01:18:16.518 --> 01:18:20.066
there's a lot more fails than there are successes. And I want to make sure

01:18:20.566 --> 01:18:23.262
that we're hedging our bets if we make a decision like this to make sure

01:18:23.762 --> 01:18:23.920
we're covered.

01:18:33.237 --> 01:18:37.106
Um, I, you know, I truly appreciate the, the council's,

01:18:37.797 --> 01:18:41.445
um, one, investment into looking into

01:18:41.945 --> 01:18:45.309
this, and two, your perspective on this, because whatever you do

01:18:45.809 --> 01:18:48.095
as a council, you want to make sure it's going to enhance the quality of

01:18:48.595 --> 01:18:50.994
life of the residents that live here. There, there's just no doubt about it.

01:18:51.673 --> 01:18:54.838
I also think that we, um, we have a great opportunity ahead

01:18:55.338 --> 01:18:59.064
of us. Um, uh, we have the Pringles here from West West Desert

01:18:59.564 --> 01:19:03.243
Air Park, and we we can work together in our Cedar Valley and

01:19:03.324 --> 01:19:07.438
look at opportunities where what are they doing really well and

01:19:07.552 --> 01:19:10.937
and what can we do to help enhance them over there and

01:19:11.437 --> 01:19:14.906
what can we do to complement and and serve each other and

01:19:14.954 --> 01:19:19.381
work together to create the jobs that we need and to be able to really

01:19:19.478 --> 01:19:23.145
advance the future of aviation.

01:19:23.804 --> 01:19:26.257
I'm listening to a book right now. on leadership.

01:19:26.673 --> 01:19:30.247
And, um, and as I was

01:19:30.279 --> 01:19:32.699
walking my dog, um, last night,

01:19:33.228 --> 01:19:36.272
it came across the part where, um, it was,

01:19:36.529 --> 01:19:40.134
it was talking about, um, uh, Franklin Delano

01:19:40.199 --> 01:19:43.564
Roosevelt. And he was the first president that, that used the

01:19:43.644 --> 01:19:47.554
automobile to help, um, secure his position.

01:19:48.452 --> 01:19:51.738
And one of the things that he did, um, because it

01:19:52.238 --> 01:19:55.574
was perceived, hey, this is the automobile, farmers were upset, hey, don't,

01:19:56.007 --> 01:19:58.446
you know, you're scaring my animals and all that type of thing.

01:19:59.137 --> 01:20:02.330
It was perceived as being something that, you know,

01:20:02.347 --> 01:20:05.524
the rich or the hoity-toity or whatever would do. Are you sure you want to

01:20:05.540 --> 01:20:08.814
do this on this election? And his

01:20:08.911 --> 01:20:11.912
thing is he wanted to be mobile. He wanted to get out there and talk

01:20:12.412 --> 01:20:15.603
to every single resident that he possibly could. But he also

01:20:16.103 --> 01:20:19.569
mitigated that by ensuring that his driver would

01:20:19.601 --> 01:20:22.716
pull over every single time they saw a horse, and then he would talk to

01:20:23.216 --> 01:20:25.783
the person who was pulling that wagon and to the horse.

01:20:26.650 --> 01:20:29.123
You know, when we look at the automobile industry,

01:20:30.343 --> 01:20:34.293
it was, it was a newfangled way

01:20:34.793 --> 01:20:38.195
of doing things. Cars were interrupting with horses. There was,

01:20:39.094 --> 01:20:42.562
you know, traffic issues, signal lights, all the rules that

01:20:42.642 --> 01:20:46.094
come with MUTCD that we have to deal with today on roads and

01:20:46.594 --> 01:20:50.429
technology had to evolve to get to where they are today. And I think

01:20:50.929 --> 01:20:54.492
the same thing happens in aviation. You know, we had the Wright brothers and

01:20:55.150 --> 01:20:59.068
all the different technologies that brought us to the FAA and the regulations

01:20:59.164 --> 01:21:02.809
that we have today that make it as safe and the safest type of

01:21:03.309 --> 01:21:06.646
travel that we enjoy. We also recognize that, you know,

01:21:07.352 --> 01:21:10.809
there are impacts. You know, if you live right next to a very loud

01:21:10.906 --> 01:21:14.191
airport, that can be very impactful

01:21:14.239 --> 01:21:18.575
to a person's life. But the evolving technologies are extremely

01:21:18.607 --> 01:21:22.067
quiet because they're not combustion engines. They're electric.

01:21:22.649 --> 01:21:27.084
There's different things that can help mitigate. I think what

01:21:27.584 --> 01:21:30.606
I'm really getting at is that I believe

01:21:30.638 --> 01:21:33.163
personally that this technology is going to come.

01:21:34.203 --> 01:21:37.525
And, um, just like the, the automobile

01:21:37.638 --> 01:21:41.011
came, and we have a great opportunity ahead of

01:21:41.511 --> 01:21:44.755
us to, um, to one, be the, um,

01:21:45.590 --> 01:21:49.205
the city that has a seat at the table that really conceives how to

01:21:49.705 --> 01:21:53.286
do it right, how to think about it, how to mitigate the impacts, how to

01:21:53.786 --> 01:21:57.367
consider locations so that it would be less impactful, how to

01:21:57.479 --> 01:22:01.384
listen to our residents, how to have open houses, how to have clear communication,

01:22:01.978 --> 01:22:04.839
but also to do it in such a way that we can create the jobs

01:22:05.032 --> 01:22:07.972
that perhaps Detroit created back when automobiles,

01:22:09.355 --> 01:22:12.870
you know, came forward. How do we diversify? How do

01:22:13.370 --> 01:22:16.661
we get away from just one type of industry to multiple? And how does

01:22:16.774 --> 01:22:20.742
aviation potentially help us when it comes to things like cargo shipments

01:22:21.256 --> 01:22:24.485
and delivering cookies and

01:22:24.985 --> 01:22:27.779
cream ice cream from Walmart to your home or Wendy's to, you know,

01:22:28.645 --> 01:22:32.815
the Ubers of tomorrow, that we recognize that a 45-minute

01:22:33.315 --> 01:22:36.888
drive to the Provo Airport is about a 4 to 5 minute flight to get

01:22:37.388 --> 01:22:40.240
there on an Uber-type aircraft in the future.

01:22:40.898 --> 01:22:44.795
And we recognize that, that things will change and things will evolve, but how

01:22:44.907 --> 01:22:48.098
can we do it in such a way that meets the needs of our community

01:22:48.836 --> 01:22:52.509
and do so with eyes wide open? The study

01:22:53.530 --> 01:22:57.506
research, to plan it out properly so that we don't have it run over top

01:22:58.006 --> 01:23:01.180
of us, but we can be at the forefront in leadership at the state level

01:23:01.680 --> 01:23:05.147
to ensure that it's a beneficial impact to our community

01:23:05.244 --> 01:23:06.205
and not a negative impact.

01:23:10.342 --> 01:23:14.346
Similar to what— the only thing that I was going to say is we're

01:23:15.730 --> 01:23:18.889
already seeing the piece that wasn't really touched on is the

01:23:18.938 --> 01:23:21.929
cargo operations. It's already been tested. It's coming.

01:23:22.835 --> 01:23:25.990
You're going to have drones flying in Eagle Mountain dropping cargo whether we

01:23:26.490 --> 01:23:29.893
have a vertiport or not. So we're not going to protect

01:23:30.393 --> 01:23:32.738
the airspace whether or not we have an airport.

01:23:33.708 --> 01:23:36.130
They're going to be flying in and out of here.

01:23:37.422 --> 01:23:40.555
The other piece obviously is the Air National Guard.

01:23:41.557 --> 01:23:44.966
We have a great opportunity with good partners. I was just going to

01:23:45.466 --> 01:23:49.754
counter the one point about corporate jets. They're landing a long ways away

01:23:50.561 --> 01:23:53.420
to go to Salt Lake because they can't get into the airports now.

01:23:54.634 --> 01:23:57.515
There is a need for corporate jets. We've seen, seen that.

01:23:58.095 --> 01:24:00.751
Doesn't mean we have to capture that. It doesn't matter. It's up to us.

01:24:00.848 --> 01:24:03.939
It's just a matter of— I just wanted to point out, I've been going to

01:24:03.955 --> 01:24:07.475
all these meetings with Joby, and they're already flying.

01:24:09.098 --> 01:24:12.682
They landed in Salt Lake the other day. I think you came up to one

01:24:12.730 --> 01:24:15.951
of those. We don't have

01:24:16.451 --> 01:24:19.788
to do this, but it's going to happen around us whether

01:24:20.288 --> 01:24:23.626
we want to be a part of it and capture some of that and kind

01:24:23.802 --> 01:24:27.736
of guide how it goes or just

01:24:28.236 --> 01:24:30.931
let it happen around us and watch it.

01:24:32.072 --> 01:24:35.459
Yeah, I think just where you

01:24:35.959 --> 01:24:40.052
nest an airport, that is a very big decision. And if it's nestled

01:24:40.552 --> 01:24:44.130
next to homes, I have big issues with that. If we put it south end

01:24:44.630 --> 01:24:48.257
of the Cedar Valley somewhere in county property, great. Like,

01:24:48.305 --> 01:24:51.677
go ahead and build an airport out there. That makes more sense.

01:24:52.239 --> 01:24:55.498
So I think the location, if we are doing fixed wing, because they are loud,

01:24:55.916 --> 01:24:59.689
they are noisy when they take off. Some of the drones are more quiet.

01:25:00.733 --> 01:25:04.794
That's more what I'm going to wrestle with is People,

01:25:05.948 --> 01:25:08.784
you know, us not mitigating that, um,

01:25:09.329 --> 01:25:13.271
at least as the noise volumes go up, I just have concerns

01:25:13.896 --> 01:25:17.614
around that piece. I think we can, uh, I don't want

01:25:18.207 --> 01:25:22.037
to say dictate, we can control some of that. Like, they're already landing fixed wings

01:25:22.133 --> 01:25:25.835
over here, right? They're little Cessnas or whatever. You watch them. We don't have to

01:25:25.979 --> 01:25:29.488
land corporate jets, but the military flies

01:25:29.585 --> 01:25:32.844
out here Almost daily. I mean, we can't

01:25:33.344 --> 01:25:35.410
stop that. That's kind of my point, is to say, oh, we don't want an

01:25:35.910 --> 01:25:39.540
airport because we don't want that. It's already happening. They're already doing it.

01:25:39.588 --> 01:25:43.207
So do we want corporate jets? Maybe, maybe not. We don't

01:25:43.707 --> 01:25:47.135
have to house corporate jets because that would be part of it, is the,

01:25:47.281 --> 01:25:51.620
the hangar space. Uh, but the drones and the helicopters

01:25:52.120 --> 01:25:55.235
are already here. I would counter that though.

01:25:55.460 --> 01:25:57.708
I don't know that that means we have put our hands in the air and

01:25:58.208 --> 01:26:00.949
surrender and say, it's coming anyway, so just come drop it here.

01:26:01.723 --> 01:26:04.415
And I think that a lot of citizens probably feel that way too.

01:26:06.046 --> 01:26:10.205
You're probably right. We're probably going to see some cargo drones

01:26:10.237 --> 01:26:14.046
coming in. There's a big difference between that and takeoffs

01:26:14.546 --> 01:26:18.064
and landings constantly when you have an aviation school, as an example.

01:26:18.144 --> 01:26:21.910
That was one of the examples that was brought here. And I told Zach

01:26:21.975 --> 01:26:25.478
this before we got here. I would challenge anybody who has not sat at the

01:26:25.591 --> 01:26:28.429
Heber City Airport before you make a decision.

01:26:29.215 --> 01:26:32.021
In fact, I would tell you not to make a decision until you go and

01:26:32.037 --> 01:26:36.335
sit there and listen. Spend an hour on a Saturday and

01:26:36.415 --> 01:26:40.167
listen how many times the planes take off and land there, and then

01:26:40.263 --> 01:26:43.807
tell me that there's no impact. The reality of it is, is there's a

01:26:44.307 --> 01:26:47.977
big difference between it's coming and there's going to be an impact

01:26:48.477 --> 01:26:51.161
down the, down the road and saying Let's put it here.

01:26:54.167 --> 01:26:58.023
To Zach's point, putting it in an area that's, you know,

01:26:58.104 --> 01:27:02.345
where we already have a large industrial presence is a very different conversation

01:27:02.940 --> 01:27:06.692
than putting it next to our large lots where we've tried

01:27:06.949 --> 01:27:10.702
to create a kind of a unique area up there where

01:27:10.750 --> 01:27:14.076
we're planning to put future regional parks for people to come and

01:27:14.109 --> 01:27:18.332
visit. I just—

01:27:18.974 --> 01:27:22.075
the 2 decisions can be separate from each

01:27:22.171 --> 01:27:25.850
other. I think the location that has been talked about is

01:27:25.947 --> 01:27:29.658
giving me the, I don't want to do this, because I think the location's

01:27:30.158 --> 01:27:33.915
a bad location. I think that if we contemplate this in an area where

01:27:34.415 --> 01:27:38.220
we already have very few houses, there's never going to really be that because

01:27:38.236 --> 01:27:41.241
we plan that way. Very different conversation.

01:27:41.321 --> 01:27:45.092
I mean, again, the Salt Lake Airport Now has houses that are getting close

01:27:45.592 --> 01:27:48.683
to it, but when they did it, they built it away from everything. And they

01:27:49.183 --> 01:27:53.075
built a lot of industrial uses around that to create that

01:27:53.107 --> 01:27:57.275
same environment to keep it separate. The Provo Airport, same thing.

01:27:57.356 --> 01:28:01.684
It's out by the lake. It's away from houses. This is a different conversation.

01:28:01.748 --> 01:28:05.083
We're literally trying to put it next to areas where there are houses

01:28:05.115 --> 01:28:08.449
that already exist and that they have already been planned.

01:28:10.114 --> 01:28:13.658
So, I mean, for me, that is a nonstarter. I think we

01:28:14.158 --> 01:28:17.619
need to evaluate, first of all, where we think it can exist before we

01:28:18.119 --> 01:28:18.834
say it should exist.

01:28:22.286 --> 01:28:25.757
And I guess I would say that as far as location is concerned,

01:28:25.902 --> 01:28:30.486
certain locations have already been ruled out that wouldn't

01:28:30.986 --> 01:28:33.486
be wise when it comes to prevailing winds.

01:28:34.668 --> 01:28:37.074
To restricted airspace and to topography.

01:28:38.544 --> 01:28:42.904
Other areas are less impactful

01:28:42.985 --> 01:28:46.457
to our residents, and south area of town even is

01:28:46.957 --> 01:28:49.962
even more less impactful to our residents. I don't think that we are at a

01:28:50.462 --> 01:28:55.733
stage right now where we are saying

01:28:55.847 --> 01:28:58.501
this is where it has to be and we're fighting towards where it should or

01:28:59.001 --> 01:29:02.325
should not be. What we're at a stage at of of this is the

01:29:02.825 --> 01:29:06.332
possibilities. Here's the jobs that we could create. Here's the opportunities that it could

01:29:06.832 --> 01:29:09.971
mean. Here's the educational institutions that might come in.

01:29:10.243 --> 01:29:14.187
I mean, we're the Cedar Valley Aviators, for goodness'

01:29:14.687 --> 01:29:18.483
sake. We're the 2nd largest high school in the state, and I believe

01:29:18.515 --> 01:29:21.448
the only high school that you can get your private pilot's license and your drone

01:29:21.544 --> 01:29:25.071
certification in the high school. That's fantastic.

01:29:26.193 --> 01:29:29.273
And wouldn't it be great if could bring a university in that would do the

01:29:29.773 --> 01:29:33.108
same, that we could take a pipeline that creates a path for our

01:29:33.608 --> 01:29:37.361
students, um, to leave Cedar Valley High School, go into Utah

01:29:37.861 --> 01:29:41.197
State University or Utah Valley University right here in Eagle

01:29:41.697 --> 01:29:45.225
Mountain, and then go into a manufacturing or, uh,

01:29:45.626 --> 01:29:48.226
an aviation profession where they can work and live here,

01:29:48.980 --> 01:29:52.976
and we can keep our kids and grandkids here. But like all of you,

01:29:53.072 --> 01:29:56.267
I wouldn't want to have an airport right— well, maybe I would want to

01:29:56.315 --> 01:29:59.551
have an airport right behind my home because I just love aviation and I

01:29:59.616 --> 01:30:02.836
love the sound of it and, and whatnot. But, um, but,

01:30:03.353 --> 01:30:07.089
but, uh, you know, not for the detrimental

01:30:07.202 --> 01:30:10.005
aspects of life. We, we want to make sure that if we're going to do

01:30:10.021 --> 01:30:13.408
anything, we want to do it right. And so this is— we're at

01:30:13.908 --> 01:30:17.597
that easy early stage where we're trying to listen to you as a council

01:30:18.099 --> 01:30:21.557
and listen to our community at large and plan it right and

01:30:21.830 --> 01:30:25.317
plan the land use around it so it doesn't get encroached upon,

01:30:25.446 --> 01:30:28.788
so we don't have negative impacts. And, and,

01:30:29.768 --> 01:30:32.564
you know, being the 9th fastest growing city in the country,

01:30:32.757 --> 01:30:36.404
our land will get eaten up that fast. But if we plan it right now,

01:30:37.561 --> 01:30:41.032
not only could it be an exceptionally good use and a great job creator

01:30:41.532 --> 01:30:44.936
for our community, but it's actually low water use as well and

01:30:45.113 --> 01:30:48.871
would prevent perhaps, uh, inhibit large

01:30:48.967 --> 01:30:52.997
tracts of homes that might come into that same exact area that

01:30:53.497 --> 01:30:57.060
would further impact our roads and whatnot. So creating jobs,

01:30:57.156 --> 01:30:59.950
creating, um, um, opportunities,

01:31:00.207 --> 01:31:03.964
and, and, uh, utilizing that space and planning wisely is

01:31:04.060 --> 01:31:07.352
the stage that we're at right now. Nobody's trying to impose anything on anybody at

01:31:07.852 --> 01:31:08.974
this point.

01:31:11.599 --> 01:31:15.396
All right, I think we're good as far as this portion, right?

01:31:16.601 --> 01:31:20.261
Mayor, can I make one thing? We, um, are hosting an open

01:31:20.278 --> 01:31:23.778
house for residents to also weigh in, learn about it a little bit more,

01:31:24.533 --> 01:31:28.547
um, and we really do invite them and also you as well to come and

01:31:29.047 --> 01:31:31.935
listen to the residents. What, what are their concerns? What do they like? What do

01:31:32.435 --> 01:31:35.772
they not like? We do have— we're going to have about 5 stations where

01:31:36.272 --> 01:31:39.284
we talk about different things like how do we— how are we looking at protecting

01:31:39.380 --> 01:31:42.811
wildlife and the noise and land? And then also,

01:31:43.709 --> 01:31:47.219
you know, what are some jobs that could come out of it? But there's one

