02:30:02.852 --> 02:30:06.365
Hopefully. My dad, Todd Black, had a deep

02:30:06.865 --> 02:30:10.042
love for the outdoors— I already started, not a good

02:30:10.138 --> 02:30:13.799
start— and a genuine passion for understanding and protecting

02:30:13.895 --> 02:30:18.086
the natural world. Raised in southeastern Utah, he turned that passion

02:30:18.586 --> 02:30:22.149
into a lifelong career of wildlife biology, conservation, and land

02:30:22.181 --> 02:30:25.344
management. An Aggie and BYU graduate,

02:30:25.472 --> 02:30:27.897
my dad dedicated his career to studying wildlife.

02:30:28.464 --> 02:30:32.197
Preserving open spaces and helping others better understand the world around him.

02:30:33.199 --> 02:30:37.048
He worked with Utah State University, Desert Land and Livestock,

02:30:37.456 --> 02:30:41.041
and Eagle Mountain City as wildlife biologist and open land manager.

02:30:41.890 --> 02:30:44.549
My dad had an incredible depth of knowledge,

02:30:45.854 --> 02:30:48.771
but what made him special was his desire to share it.

02:30:49.657 --> 02:30:53.780
Whether studying wildlife, managing land, or teaching

02:30:53.812 --> 02:30:57.553
others, He approached everything with curiosity,

02:30:57.649 --> 02:31:01.120
purpose, and a genuine love for the outdoors. On behalf

02:31:01.620 --> 02:31:05.346
of my family, we would love to thank Eagle Mountain City for loving my dad

02:31:05.458 --> 02:31:08.881
and giving him the opportunity to pursue his passion of

02:31:08.913 --> 02:31:12.833
wildlife and conservation. We would also like to thank the Wildlife

02:31:12.849 --> 02:31:16.304
Conservation Alliance for their tireless work and as they

02:31:16.804 --> 02:31:20.707
continue his legacy, and for Utah State University and their efforts and

02:31:20.804 --> 02:31:24.785
opening the doors for future generations of wildlife preservation through higher

02:31:24.833 --> 02:31:26.091
education. Thank you.

